+---
+id: dl-004-bulk-import-reuses-tasklist.add-s-validation-reads-the-clock-once-at-the-index.ts-boundary
+type: decision-log
+title: "Bulk import reuses TaskList.add's validation, reads the clock once at the index.ts boundary"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Teams moving from spreadsheets need a bulk import: `importTasks(list, text)`, one task per line as
+`title;priority;tags`, with `priority` optional and `tags` space-separated. Its signature, fixed by the
+request, takes only `list` and `text`, unlike every `TaskList` method that mutates the list, which takes
+an explicit `now` so that `src/domain/` (bound by `r3-no-clock-or-randomness`) never reads the clock
+itself.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `importTasks` lives in `src/index.ts`, not `src/domain/`: it is the one place in this change that
+  reads `new Date().toISOString()`, once per call, then passes that same `now` to every `list.add` in
+  the batch. `r3` only binds `src/domain/`, so this stays compliant while giving `importTasks` the
+  two-argument signature asked for.
+- Each line is split on `;` into at most three fields; `priority` and `tags` default to empty when a
+  line omits the trailing fields entirely (e.g. a bare title with no `;`), since spreadsheet exports
+  often drop empty trailing columns. More than three fields is rejected outright, as that is more likely
+  a stray `;` in the data than a trailing-field omission.
+- No separate validation is written for `importTasks`: each parsed line is handed to `TaskList.add`
+  unchanged, and a failure's `error.message` becomes the line's `reason`. This reuses the title/priority/
+  tag validation and the duplicate-title check `add` already has (including duplicates against a title
+  imported from an earlier line in the same batch), rather than duplicating those rules.
+- A line that is blank (empty, or only whitespace) is skipped silently and does not appear in `rejected`;
+  a line with content that still fails to produce a valid task (e.g. an empty title between two `;`) is
+  rejected with its 1-based line number.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `importTasks`'s timestamps are not individually injectable from the call site, so its tests check
+  behaviour (what got imported, what got rejected, the resulting tasks' title/priority/tags) rather than
+  exact `createdAt`/`updatedAt` values; this is the one function in the public API whose output is not
+  fully deterministic given its inputs alone.
+- Every task added in one `importTasks` call shares the same `createdAt`/`updatedAt` moment.
+- `RejectedLine.reason` is whatever message `TaskList.add` (or the too-many-fields check) produces, so a
+  change to those messages changes import's rejection text too; there is only one place each message is
+  written.
